   "id": "S1",
   "kind": "document",
   "title": "Data Wrangling Handbook",
   "text": "4. Grouping and Aggregation\ngroupby splits rows into groups, applies an aggregate such as sum or mean to each group and\ncombines the results. In SQL, WHERE filters individual rows before grouping, while HAVING filters\nwhole groups after aggregation. The logical order of clauses is FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY,\nHAVING, SELECT, ORDER BY, LIMIT.",
   "doc_id": "data_wrangling_handbook",
   "course_id": "data",
   "location": "p.4",
   "chunk_id": "data_wrangling_handbook:0003",
   "score": 0.4047722816467285,
   "data": {
    "file_type": "pdf",
    "page": 4,
    "section": "4. Grouping and Aggregation",
    "filename": "data_wrangling_handbook.pdf"
   },
   "derived_from": []
  },
  {
   "id": "S8",
   "kind": "document",
   "title": "Data Wrangling Handbook",
   "text": "3. Combining Tables\npd.merge joins two DataFrames on key columns. The default is an inner join, which keeps only\nkeys present in both tables. A left join keeps every row of the left table and fills unmatched\nright-hand columns with NaN.\nIf a key is duplicated in both tables the result contains every pairing of the matching rows, so the\nrow count can grow (a many-to-many join). pd.concat stacks tables; by default it stacks vertically\n(axis=0).",
   "doc_id": "data_wrangling_handbook",
   "course_id": "data",
   "location": "p.3",
   "chunk_id": "data_wrangling_handbook:0002",
